# name: Local network
# source: NodePassProject/Somewhere-Android
# revision: this repository
# licence: GPL-3.0-only
# date: 2026-08-29
#
# What a tunnel must not swallow.
#
# Every address here means "somewhere on this side of the tunnel": the router's
# admin page, a printer, a NAS, a phone talking to a laptop over the same
# Wi-Fi. Sending them to a Portal reaches nothing, slowly — the Portal has its
# own 192.168.0.0/16 and it is not yours.
#
# It names no country and no service, and that is deliberate rather than
# incidental. A rule set that named either would be a political claim shipped
# under this project's name, in a public history that cannot really be deleted,
# by a client whose stated posture is mechanisms without embedded policy. This
# one is arithmetic about address space.
#
# The fake-IP range 198.18.0.0/15 is deliberately absent. A synthetic address is
# how a name survives the trip from the resolver to the flow handler, and a
# DIRECT rule over it would be a second, contradictory statement of something
# the router already guarantees: a direct flow dials the name, never the address
# that carried it.

# This device.
IP-CIDR,127.0.0.0/8,DIRECT
IP-CIDR,0.0.0.0/8,DIRECT

# RFC 1918. The private ranges, which is most of what "the local network" means.
IP-CIDR,10.0.0.0/8,DIRECT
IP-CIDR,172.16.0.0/12,DIRECT
IP-CIDR,192.168.0.0/16,DIRECT

# RFC 6598. Carrier-grade NAT: not the local network, but not routable to a
# Portal either, and a mobile network's own infrastructure lives here.
IP-CIDR,100.64.0.0/10,DIRECT

# RFC 3927. Link-local, which is what a device uses when nothing gave it an
# address — including the Android emulator's own host alias.
IP-CIDR,169.254.0.0/16,DIRECT

# RFC 5771 and the limited broadcast. Neither survives a tunnel by definition.
IP-CIDR,224.0.0.0/4,DIRECT
IP-CIDR,255.255.255.255/32,DIRECT

# RFC 6890's documentation and benchmarking ranges. Nothing real is behind
# them, and a flow that reaches one is a misconfiguration worth failing fast.
IP-CIDR,192.0.2.0/24,DIRECT
IP-CIDR,198.51.100.0/24,DIRECT
IP-CIDR,203.0.113.0/24,DIRECT

# Names that only ever mean this network. `.local` is mDNS; `.home.arpa` is
# RFC 8375; `.lan` and `.internal` are conventions every home router uses.
DOMAIN-SUFFIX,local,DIRECT
DOMAIN-SUFFIX,localhost,DIRECT
DOMAIN-SUFFIX,home.arpa,DIRECT
DOMAIN-SUFFIX,lan,DIRECT
DOMAIN-SUFFIX,internal,DIRECT
